// The `prelude.env` sub-module: project-scoped environment access. These are host primitives bound to
// the project's `env_entries` store when the runtime boots. Called as `env.get_secret` / `env.get_or`.
// The split is the module: a secret entry reads one key at a time and throws when unset, a non-secret
// entry reads through a total reader whose @fallback@ is the value an unset knob means.

@"No secret env entry is set under @key@. Thrown by `get_secret` — catch it to fall back in-program
(`use handler { request throw(error: env.missing_secret) -> never { ... } }`, or fold it to a value with
`prelude.catch`)."
data missing_secret(@"The key that had no secret entry." key: string, message: string)

@"Read a secret env entry by key, returning its value as a private string (tainted secret: it cannot
flow to a user-facing boundary). A missing key throws `missing_secret` carrying the key; uncaught, the
throw fails the run."
primitive agent get_secret(@"The secret entry's key in the project's env store." key: string) -> string of private with prelude.throw[missing_secret]

@"Read one non-secret env entry by key, answering @fallback@ when no entry is set — total, so there is
nothing to catch and nothing to branch on. Secrets are not visible here; they read through
`get_secret`, which taints its answer private. An entry set to the empty string comes back as the
empty string, not as @fallback@."
primitive agent get_or(
  @"The non-secret entry's key in the project's env store." key: string,
  @"The value when no entry is set under @key@." fallback: string,
) -> string
